+// The i8 kernels walk 128 values (one stage) over 64 weight rows, and one tile's rows are `row_bytes` apart in the blob: a warp's
+// 16-byte read therefore lands in up to eight 128-byte lines of eight different rows, and a stage's bytes of a row are re-fetched
+// from a line that half of it (a super-block holds two stages) does not belong to this stage.  The packed image lays a tile's stage
+// out as [64 rows][record], so one (tile, stage) is a contiguous 5-7 KB block: 64 rows x i8_pk_rb() bytes.  Records, 16-byte
+// aligned (d = the record's stage bytes; every stage carries the whole super-block header because the kernel's decode reads it):
+//   Q4_K  80: header 16 (d, dmin, 12 scale bytes) | 64 code bytes of this stage's two sub-block pairs
+//   Q5_K 112: header 16 | the super-block's 32 qh bytes (bit ib of every byte is read by every stage) | 64 code bytes
+//   Q5_1  96: the stage's four 24-byte blocks, already contiguous in the row (a pure copy)
+//   Q8_0 144: the stage's four 34-byte blocks (136) | pad 8
+// The packed image is a SECOND copy of an expert's weights: the decode kernels (native_gu_kernel / native_down_kernel, row_dot)
+// keep reading the original blob unchanged, and only native_expert_i8 reads the packed one (its `pk_base` argument).  The model
